course: everyday
lesson: 330
title: Forgotten lunch, lunch rubbish, a classmate with no lunch, learning to cook, pocket money, southern seasons, jobs and salaries, and festivals
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: At lunchtime problems are easy to solve if you tell an adult: if you forget your lunch, or a classmate has no lunch or very little, a teacher or the office can help.
explain: A classmate with no lunch should be helped privately: tell a teacher quietly, because many schools offer free school meals and nobody should be embarrassed.
explain: Money comes from work: adults earn a salary at a job, and that money pays for the home, food and bills; it does not come from the card machine. Your pocket money or allowance is worth keeping safe and keeping track of.
explain: Example: a girl in Sydney has a barbecue on the beach on Christmas Day, because in Australia and New Zealand summer is from December to February.
explain: Respect the festivals your classmates celebrate, such as Christmas, Diwali, Eid, Hanukkah or Lunar New Year: everyone's traditions deserve respect.
fact: If you forget your lunch, tell the teacher or the office; the school can phone home or provide something to eat.
fact: Put leftover lunch rubbish in the right bin: recycling, compost or general waste.
fact: If someone has no lunch or very little, tell a teacher quietly rather than helping in a way that embarrasses them.
fact: Many schools offer free school meals, so the school can help a child privately.
fact: When learning to cook, cook with an adult, wash your hands and be careful with knives and hot pans.
fact: Keep pocket money or an allowance in a safe place, such as a money box or purse, and keep track of how much you have.
fact: In Australia and New Zealand summer is from December to February, so Christmas is in summer; the seasons are opposite in the southern hemisphere.
fact: A job is work that adults do to earn money, called a salary or wages, which pays for the home, food and bills.
fact: Money comes from work, not from the card machine or the cash machine.
fact: Be respectful when classmates celebrate Christmas, Diwali, Eid, Hanukkah or Lunar New Year.
exercise: You get to school and realise you forgot your lunch. What should you do? | Tell the teacher or the office | Go without and say nothing | Take food from someone's bag | Walk home alone to fetch it
exercise: Why tell an adult when you forget your lunch? | The school can phone home or give you something to eat | You will be sent home for the rest of the day to eat there | Teachers keep spare sweets for you | It is against the rules to eat at school
exercise: After lunch you have an apple core, a clean juice carton and a crisp packet. What do you do? | Put each in the right bin: compost, recycling or general waste | Put everything together in the nearest bin, since it all goes to the same place | Leave them on the table for the cleaner | Throw them all in the compost
exercise: Why sort your lunch rubbish into the right bins? | It keeps the school clean and recycling helps the environment | It is only to keep the dinner staff busy after lunch has finished | It makes the bins heavier | Bins break if things are mixed
exercise: You notice a classmate often has no lunch or very little. What is the kind thing to do? | Tell a teacher quietly | Announce it to the class so everyone shares | Ask him loudly why he has no food | Ignore it, it is not your business
exercise: Why tell a teacher quietly about a classmate with no lunch? | The school can help privately, without embarrassing him | So the class can vote together on what everyone should give him | So the classmate gets into trouble | Teachers like to know who is hungry for fun
exercise: You want to learn to cook pasta. What is the safe way? | Cook with an adult, wash hands, be careful with knives and hot pans | Cook alone while everyone is out, so nobody gets in your way in the kitchen | Use the sharpest knife as fast as you can | Hold the hot pan with bare hands
exercise: Why should a child learn to cook with an adult? | Cooking is useful, but heat and knives can hurt | Adults must taste everything first by law | Children are not allowed to eat home cooking at all | Food cooked by children goes bad faster
exercise: You get pocket money (an allowance) each week. What is a good habit? | Keep it in a money box and keep track of how much you have | Hide it in different places around the house and then forget where | Carry it all loose in your coat pocket | Spend it all on the first day
exercise: Why keep track of your pocket money? | You will know what you can afford | Money grows if you count it | Banks check children's money every week | So you can spend more than you have
exercise: When is summer in Australia and New Zealand? | December to February | June to August, as in Europe | March to May | All year round
exercise: Why is Christmas in summer in New Zealand? | The seasons are opposite in the southern hemisphere | New Zealand is much closer to the Sun at Christmas time | Christmas is held on a different date there | New Zealand has no winter at all
exercise: What is a salary? | Money an adult earns for doing a job | Money the card machine gives you for free | A present the bank gives every family | Money the school pays children
exercise: Where does the money for a family's home, food and bills come from? | From adults working at jobs | From the card machine at the shop | From the cash machine, which makes it | From the government printing it for each family
exercise: A classmate tells the class about celebrating Diwali. How should you act? | Listen with respect and ask kind questions | Say your own festival is better and more fun | Laugh at the parts that seem strange | Tell her it is not a real holiday
exercise: Which festivals deserve respect in a class? | Christmas, Diwali, Eid, Hanukkah, Lunar New Year and others | Only the festival that most people in the class celebrate at home | Only festivals with presents | Only the ones on the school calendar
test: A boy's little sister asks, «Why don't we just get more money from the card machine?» What is the truest answer? | The money in the bank comes from work, the machine only gives out what was earned | The machine prints new money for anyone who puts a card in and types the number | Money in the machine is free once a month | The shop pays for anything bought by card
test: A girl in Ohio talks to her pen pal in Auckland in July. What season is it in Auckland? | Winter | Summer | Spring | Autumn
test: Which is the best way to help a classmate who never has lunch? | Quietly tell a teacher, who can arrange help such as free school meals | Collect food from the whole class in front of him so he can see who cares | Tell everyone at break time | Give him your lunch every day and say nothing to anyone
test: A boy keeps his allowance loose in his schoolbag and never knows how much he has. What should he change? | Keep it in a money box and write down what he has | Spend it faster so it cannot get lost | Ask for more money each week | Keep it in his shoes
test: A girl wants to make pancakes alone while her mum is out. What is wise? | Wait and make them with an adult | Make them quickly before Mum is back | Use the highest heat to save time | Ask a younger brother to help instead
test: Lunch is over in a school in Leeds. What happens to a banana skin? | It goes in the compost or food waste bin | It goes in the paper recycling with the cartons | It is left on the table | It is thrown on the field for birds
test: A boy in Auckland forgot his lunch and his mum is at work. Who should he go to? | A teacher or the school office | No one, he should just wait there | A classmate's parent at the gate | The shop across the road, on his own
test: During Eid, a classmate brings in sweets to share. What is respectful? | Say thank you and take one or politely say no thanks | Refuse rudely because it is not your festival and you don't have to | Laugh at the way the sweets look | Tell her to keep her festival at home
explain: A girl in England had no lunch and was too embarrassed to say; she felt faint in the afternoon, and when a teacher found out the school helped quietly.
explain: A boy in Canada cooked pasta on his own and tipped the pan; the boiling water splashed his legs and he was burned.
fact: Boiling water causes many children's burns, especially when pans are tipped or pulled down.
fact: Turn pan handles in so they do not stick out over the edge of the cooker, where someone can knock or pull them.
fact: Schools can help a child who has no food without others knowing.
test: my mate never has lunch and is ashamed | Tell a teacher quietly | Tell the whole class | Laugh it off | Ignore it
test: making pasta on my own while mum's out | Cook only with an adult | Fine, just be really careful | Fine if you use a big pan | Fine if you wear oven gloves
test: pan handle sticking out over the edge | Turn it in so no one knocks it | Leave it, it's easier to grab | Hang a towel on it | Push it further out
test: did u hear a kid pulled a pan handle on the stove and "the boiling pasta water went all over him". lesson? | Cook with an adult and be careful with hot pans | Pull pans off quickly so they don't spill over | Pasta water is never hot enough to burn | Only adults get burnt
test: my cousin says in Australia "they have Christmas on the beach cos it's always summer". what's the true bit | Christmas is in summer there because the seasons are opposite | It's summer there all year round | Christmas is in winter there | They don't have Christmas there
test: kids say the boy who gets free school meals "is a charity case". pass it on? | No, schools help privately and that's unkind | Yes, he's poor, so it's just the truth anyway | Yes, and tell everyone at lunch | Yes, it's true
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the source of money? | It comes from work, paid as a salary or wages | It comes from the card machine | It comes from the cash machine | It is printed by each family
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a teacher is told quietly when a classmate has no lunch. | The school can help without embarrassing them | Teachers must punish the parents of such pupils | Classmates are forbidden from sharing | It is required by the cook
exam: It is given that a pupil has forgotten their lunch. Select the response consistent with established guidance. | The pupil tells the teacher or the office | The pupil goes without food all day | The pupil takes another pupil's lunch | The pupil walks home to fetch it
exam: State the months of summer in Australia and New Zealand. | December to February | June to August | March to May | September to November
